---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RailIndex from '../components/RailIndex.astro';

const reviewed = '19 September 2026';

// Os títulos têm de bater LITERALMENTE com os <h2> do corpo — checagem 22.
const sections = [
  { id: 'stored', title: 'Then what is stored?' },
  { id: 'analytics', title: 'Analytics' },
  { id: 'would-change', title: 'What would change this page' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Cookies — mazetick"
  description="mazetick sets no cookies, first-party or third-party. What it does store, and the difference between a cookie and local storage."
>
  <header class="mast">
    <p class="mono kicker">Notice</p>
    <h1 class="display title">Cookies</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="spread">
    <div class="body">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>This site sets no cookies.</strong> Not our own, not anyone else's.
          There is no consent banner because there is nothing to consent to.
        </p>

        <h2 id="stored">Then what is stored?</h2>
        <p>
          At most one entry in your browser's <strong>local storage</strong>, named{" "}
          <code>mazetick:theme</code>, whose value is <code>light</code> or{" "}
          <code>dark</code>. It records that you chose a colour theme by hand, so the
          site can honour that choice when you come back.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you have never pressed the theme control, <strong>nothing is stored at
          all</strong> and the site simply follows the light or dark setting your
          operating system already reports to every website. Choosing
          &ldquo;Auto&rdquo; again deletes the entry.
        </p>

        <h3>Why that is not a cookie</h3>
        <p>
          The distinction is worth stating rather than hiding behind. A cookie is
          attached to every request your browser makes to the server, so the server sees
          it. Local storage is not: it stays on your device and is readable only by this
          site's own pages, running in your browser. Our server never receives the value
          and has no way to ask for it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the UK's Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations, storing
          information on your device requires consent unless it is strictly necessary for
          a service you requested — and a user-interface preference you set yourself is
          the textbook example of the exemption. It is stored only once you press the
          control, and never before.
        </p>

        <h3>How to remove it</h3>
        <p>
          Press &ldquo;Auto&rdquo; on the theme control, clear site data for this domain
          in your browser settings, or use a private window. The site works identically
          without it and simply follows your system's colour setting.
        </p>

        <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
        <p>
          We use Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is cookieless and does not fingerprint
          your browser. It is described in full on the{" "}
          <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="would-change">What would change this page</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Advertising of any kind, including Google AdSense, which sets cookies.</li>
          <li>Affiliate or referral links, which commonly rely on cookies to attribute a click.</li>
          <li>Any embedded third-party content — video, maps, social widgets.</li>
          <li>Any analytics product other than the cookieless one named above.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If any of those arrive, this page and the consent position have to be revised
          first. That is the point of writing the list down now.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="rail">
      <div class="rail-block">
        <span class="rail-label">As of</span>
        <p class="rail-note"><span class="stamp">Last reviewed {reviewed}</span></p>
      </div>
      <RailIndex items={sections} />
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
